{"kind":"report","text":"Re-review of notes@d0874d59 against review d6243a36 (R1-R3, advisories A1-A5), the operator's immediate-value criterion (asserts 6f239739 and be4bd61a), and the request conditions. Verdict: approved. Working tree verified clean at the exact head d0874d5.\n\nREQUIRED CHANGES — all resolved.\n\nR1 (activation compatibility): resolved beyond the minimum ask. Continue is now defined by stored writable-table shape — every existing writable table keeps identical columns, types, constraints, and primary key; new empty tables are additive-safe; event, normalizer, fold, index, view, and export changes are continue-safe with indexes and views rebuilt at the boundary under the activation journal. The activations relation carries storage_schema_digest, milestone D exercises each continue-safe class plus the refused destructive change, and acceptance 11-12 pin it. Policy-as-code iteration on agent-guard can therefore never force a data-destroying reset, which is exactly what the immediate-value story needed.\n\nR2 (read-boundary semantics): resolved. The architecture's Application memory section now states the same transaction-visibility contract as implementation section 10 — normalizer from committed state at the previous source-record boundary, normalizer changes visible to the analytic stage in the same transaction, each analytic fold seeing its own earlier changes across repeated otel_event values.\n\nR3 (obligation states): resolved. skipped is eliminated; consumed (complete transaction committed, including ineffective and zero-emission outcomes) and detached (gapped, reset, deactivated, deleted) are precisely defined, and architecture decision 3 matches.\n\nADVISORIES — all addressed with explicit decisions. A1: the single otel_event type is now named in deferred scope and non-goals as an intentional v1 simplification, not an architectural requirement. A2: the permanent gap-to-repair loss window is stated plainly in both notes and skip-and-count is explicitly deferred. A3: head-of-line blocking is acknowledged with its exporter-drop consequence, and operational status gains per-tailapp latency and oldest outstanding position. A4: the profile-upgrade flow is fully specified — control and query stay up, OTLP readiness held closed until every active tailapp is revalidated and continued, reset, or deactivated — with acceptance 17 testing it. A5: MANY reads now require an ORDER BY ending in a declared unique key, with a milestone-A refusal test.\n\nIMMEDIATE-VALUE ASSESSMENT (request condition): the bundled agent-guard tailapp gives honest immediate detective value. Cross-harness normalization into one vocabulary (harness, session identity, operation kind, tool, target, argument digest, result, event time, progress fingerprint, source position); telemetry_coverage / session_progress / policy_findings / loop_findings tables with exports; per-harness fixtures proving a known violation, an allowed operation, and an unknown-coverage case (acceptance 7). The unknown-coverage rule — absent or redacted fields must never report compliance — is the right honesty contract for default content gates, and milestone E requires gated-default fields to surface as unknown. Loop and no-progress signals are event-driven; stall detection is correctly pull-only via the caller-supplied cutoff query recipe, with the decision not to add engine tick records made explicitly. Inline enforcement is a named non-goal and the notes state the detective-controls limitation plainly, satisfying the operator's no-misrepresentation criterion.\n\nSTORAGE-COMPATIBLE ACTIVATION AND PROFILE UPGRADES (request condition): both are coherent and implementable as specified. The recoverable journal now also covers replaceable schema objects (indexes/views restored on failure before the pointer switch), and reactivating an earlier revision follows the same stored-table rules.\n\nMINOR NOTES for the implementation phase — none blocking, no draft change required:\n\nN1: acceptance 9 still says an irrelevant event is \"consumed as ineffective or unhandled\"; \"unhandled\" is a leftover — read it as the zero-emission effective case already covered by the consumed definition, or tighten the wording when the acceptance script is written.\n\nN2: holding OTLP readiness closed during a profile upgrade protects Tailapp's integrity, but harness exporters with bounded retries will drop telemetry during that window — the upgrade window is a harness-side loss window, and user documentation should say so rather than only that Tailapp does not discard it.\n\nN3: user documentation should state the end-to-end observation latency (harness export batching plus wave processing) so agent-guard queries are read as recent history, not current state — consistent with the detective-controls framing already in the notes.\n\nCONCLUSION: the design now has a defensible, implementable scope with the dataflow boundary intact and the immediate-value capability bundled and honestly framed. Approved at head d0874d5. Implementation under commitment #15 can proceed against this head; I will review the implementation head under the same conditions.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:3898bcab9e023d771174388d07dfc8c90b00fc75","head":"d0874d5923da09d690cf286f40e74bd42fd997d6","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:8f5aa2e1f979f0a581e82cebdcf884585a2ef6aa\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:8f5aa2e1f979f0a581e82cebdcf884585a2ef6aa","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}